A||N||4||Dan87GT||Wing stud fix question||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||04-17-2000||08:04 AM||||The wing on my 87GT had was almost stolen. In the process two of the studs were ripped out from the stantions. I have purchased new studs. Any thought on repair? I thought about filling the stantion holes with fiberglass and redrilling but hate to have the wing fly off.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Luperman||04-17-2000||08:36 AM||||Dan I had mine ripped out /off 2 weeks after I bought my car ten years ago , I got another wing and fixed the holes with fiberglass and fiberglass mat layered where the holes were and redrilled the holes , that fixed my wing remounting next was to prevent it from happening again, I used my dremel and cut slots in the fiberglass trunk sealing surface that separated the two mounting bolts on each side trunk where the wing bolts are and made a (2)pieces of  rectangular aluminum with two bolt holes used to reinforce the regular nuts and washers of the wing. nobodys getting away with that one with relative ease as before.good luck ||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||04-17-2000||09:19 AM||||I don't think he means the deck lid........<P>why can't you put epoxy in the holes and stick the studs into the epoxy. Would be stronger than filling holes and threading them. You'll want to mark exact location with a china marker on the sides of the standchion. You'll only get one shot at it.<P>make sure loose stuff is removed from the holes but otherwise don't do anything to them. you want the inside of the hole rough. Work the epoxy into all the little places in there with a bit of wire. Try to avoid air bubbles. (I don't know how big the holes are torn out.) Use a slower epoxy if you need time to work it in good and get out air. Something with a 15-20 minute set time is good if you're not used to working with the stuff. (5 minute epoxies start to set in around 2-3 minutes. To short a working time for most people.)<P>I'd put some fender washers on the inside of the desk lid to reinforce those holes.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan87GT||04-17-2000||10:31 AM||||Thanks guys, any suggestions on a type/brandname of epoxy||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-17-2000||07:21 PM||||brand doesn't seem to matter much.<P>Main things to look at are:<P>thermal stablity, you want one that can take some heat. most say wether or not they like high heat on the lable. The deck lid can hit 200F in the summer real easy.<P>filler, you don't need ones with metals in it, but glass or Kevlar would be good. No filler will work well to. Will be easier to get out trapped air with no filler.<P>Set time, figure it becomes unworkable at 1/3-1/2 the set time. So 5 minute stuff is workable for at most 3 minutes, likely 1.5-2 depending on temperature. This is way to short for most people. (I can work the stuff pretty quick and it's even hard for me to use 5 minute before it starts to jell.)<P>Mix enough to do one bolt at a time.<BR>Avoid whipping air into the stuff as you stir. You want as little air as posible.<BR>Use a wire to make sure the entire hole is coated. Slow set time makes this allot easier.||||reg||1
